<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Equatoria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Equatoria is a region in the far south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. It was organised as a province of the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in the 1870s, first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> and then Charles Gordon, to extend Egyptian rule up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a> and suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Its later governor, Emin Pasha, was cut off by the Mahdist revolt until rescued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> in 1889. It is now divided into three states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/equatoria.jpg" alt="Equatoria" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Equatoria_in_South_Sudan_2015.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
